   'Calculate the empirical formula of the oxide from the masses given.\nThe empirical formula of glucose is CH2O.'],
  ['Molecular Formula', 'Noun',
   '分子式。分子中の各元素の実際の原子数。実験式の整数倍になる。\nThe actual number of atoms of each element in a molecule — a whole-number multiple of the empirical formula.',
   'Deduce the molecular formula from the empirical formula and the relative molecular mass.\nThe molecular formula of ethanoic acid is C2H4O2.'],
  ['Percentage Composition by Mass', 'Expression',
   '質量パーセント組成。化合物の全質量に各元素が占める割合を百分率で表したもの。実験式計算の出発点。\nThe proportion of the total mass of a compound contributed by each element, given as a percentage.',
   'Calculate the percentage composition by mass of nitrogen in ammonium nitrate.\nThe percentage composition by mass is the usual starting point for an empirical formula.'],
  ['Actual Yield', 'Noun',
   '実収量。実験で実際に得られた生成物の質量。損失や副反応のため理論収量より必ず少ない。\nThe mass of product actually obtained from an experiment — always less than the theoretical yield.',
   'The actual yield was 3.2 g.\nSuggest two reasons why the actual yield is lower than the theoretical yield.'],
  ['Theoretical Yield', 'Noun',
   '理論収量。反応が完全に進み損失がないと仮定したときに、化学反応式から予測される生成物の質量。\nThe mass of product that the balanced equation predicts, assuming the reaction goes to completion with no losses.',
   'Calculate the theoretical yield of copper sulfate.\nThe theoretical yield assumes that no product is lost.'],
  ['Percentage Yield', 'Noun',
   '収率。実収量を理論収量で割り100を掛けた値。\nThe actual yield divided by the theoretical yield, × 100%.',
   'Calculate the percentage yield of the reaction.\nA low percentage yield may be due to an incomplete reaction.'],
  ['Atom Economy', 'Noun',
   '原子効率。反応物の質量のうち目的生成物になる割合。反応の無駄の少なさを示す。\nThe proportion of the mass of the reactants that ends up in the desired product.',
   'Calculate the atom economy for this reaction.\nAddition reactions have a high atom economy.'],
  ['Hydrated Compound', 'Noun',
   '水和物。結晶中に結晶水を含む化合物。例：硫酸銅(II)五水和物 CuSO₄·5H₂O。\nA compound whose crystals contain water of crystallisation, e.g. CuSO4·5H2O.',
   'Heat the hydrated compound until its mass is constant.\nThe hydrated compound is blue, and the anhydrous form is white.'],
  ['Anhydrous Compound', 'Noun',
   '無水物。結晶水を含まない化合物。水和物を加熱してつくる。\nA compound that contains no water of crystallisation, made by heating the hydrate.',
   'The anhydrous compound turns blue when water is added.\nWeigh the anhydrous compound after cooling in a desiccator.'],
  ['Water of Crystallisation', 'Expression',
   '結晶水。結晶構造に一定の比で取り込まれた水分子。化学式では中黒のあとに示す。\nWater molecules taken up into a crystal structure in a fixed ratio, shown after a centre dot in the formula.',
   'Calculate the number of moles of water of crystallisation in the sample.\nThe water of crystallisation is driven off on heating.'],
  ['Mass Spectrometer', 'Noun',
   '質量分析計。試料を気化・イオン化し、加速して磁場で曲げ、質量電荷比ごとに検出する装置。\nThe instrument that vaporises a sample, ionises it, accelerates and deflects the ions in a magnetic field, and detects them by mass-to-charge ratio.',
   'Describe the four stages that take place in a mass spectrometer.\nThe mass spectrometer detects each isotope separately.'],
  ['Mass Spectrum', 'Noun',
   '質量スペクトル。質量分析計の出力グラフ。縦軸は相対存在比、横軸は質量電荷比。\nThe output graph of a mass spectrometer: relative abundance against mass-to-charge ratio.',
   'Use the mass spectrum to calculate the relative atomic mass of the element.\nThe mass spectrum shows two peaks.'],
  ['Mass-to-Charge Ratio', 'Noun',
   '質量電荷比。質量スペクトルの横軸にとる量。m/e（m/z）と書き、1価イオンではイオンの質量に等しい。\nThe quantity plotted on the horizontal axis of a mass spectrum, written m/e. For singly charged ions it equals the mass of the ion.',
   'The peak at m/e = 35 is due to the chlorine-35 ion.\nState what is meant by the mass-to-charge ratio.'],
  ['Molecular Ion', 'Noun',
   '分子イオン。試料分子が電子を1個だけ失って生じるイオン M⁺。最も大きい m/e のピークが相対分子質量を与える。\nThe ion M+ formed when a whole molecule loses just one electron; its peak gives the relative molecular mass.',
   'The molecular ion peak is at m/e = 46.\nIdentify the molecular ion in the mass spectrum.'],
  ['M+1 Peak', 'Noun',
   'M+1 ピーク。分子イオンピークより1だけ大きいところに現れる小さなピーク。炭素13に由来し、その高さから炭素数がわかる。\nThe small peak one unit above the molecular ion peak, caused by carbon-13; its relative height gives the number of carbon atoms.',
   'Use the M+1 peak to deduce the number of carbon atoms in the molecule.\nThe M+1 peak is about 1.1% as high as the M peak for each carbon atom.'],
  ['Carbon-13', 'Noun',
   '炭素13。天然に存在する炭素の重い同位体（約1.1%）。M+1 ピークの原因となる。\nThe heavy, naturally occurring isotope of carbon (about 1.1% of all carbon), which produces the M+1 peak.',
   'Carbon-13 accounts for about 1.1% of all carbon atoms.\nThe M+1 peak is caused by molecules containing one carbon-13 atom.'],
  ['Fragmentation', 'Noun',
   'フラグメンテーション。高エネルギー電子が共有結合を切り、分子が小さな陽イオンに分かれること。切れやすい結合がわかる。\nThe breaking of a molecule into smaller charged pieces when high-energy electrons break its covalent bonds.',
   'Fragmentation produces peaks at lower m/e values.\nSuggest the formula of the fragment responsible for the peak at m/e = 15.'],
  ['Deflection', 'Noun',
   '偏向。運動する荷電粒子の進路が磁場や電場で曲げられること。軽いイオンほど大きく曲がる。\nThe bending of a moving charged particle’s path by a magnetic or electric field. Lighter ions are deflected more.',
   'The amount of deflection depends on the mass-to-charge ratio.\nExplain why a neutron is not deflected by the magnetic field.'],
  ['Relative Abundance', 'Noun',
   '相対存在比。各同位体がどれだけ多く存在するかを百分率やピークの高さで表したもの。\nHow common each isotope is, given as a percentage or a peak height.',
   'Multiply each isotopic mass by its relative abundance.\nThe relative abundance of chlorine-35 is 75%.'],
  ['Ideal Gas Law', 'Noun',
   '理想気体の状態方程式。pV = nRT。圧力・体積・物質量・温度を結びつける。\npV = nRT — the equation linking the pressure, volume, amount and temperature of a gas.',
   'Use the ideal gas law to calculate the volume of the gas.\nThe ideal gas law requires the temperature in kelvin.'],
  ['Gas Constant', 'Noun',
   '気体定数。R = 8.31 J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹。理想気体の状態方程式に現れる定数。\nR = 8.31 J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹ — the constant in the ideal gas equation.',
   'Substitute the gas constant into pV = nRT.\nThe gas constant has units of J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹.'],
  ['Moles Equation', 'Noun',
   '物質量の式。moles = mass ÷ molar mass（n = m / M）。変形して m = n × M、M = m / n。\nmoles = mass ÷ molar mass, i.e. n = m / M.',
   'Rearrange the moles equation to find the mass.\nUse the moles equation to convert 8.0 g of oxygen into moles.'],
  ['Kelvin', 'Noun',
   'ケルビン。絶対温度の SI 単位、記号 K。0 K が絶対零度で、度記号はつけない。\nThe SI unit of absolute temperature, symbol K. 0 K is absolute zero; there is no degree sign.',
   'Give your answer in kelvin.\nRoom temperature is about 298 K.'],
  ['Celsius to Kelvin Conversion', 'Expression',
   'セルシウス温度からケルビンへの換算。K = °C + 273。pV = nRT を使う前に必ず変換する。\nK = °C + 273 — always convert before using pV = nRT.',
   'Apply the Celsius to Kelvin conversion before substituting into the equation.\nUsing the Celsius to Kelvin conversion, 25 °C becomes 298 K.'],
  ['Pascal', 'Noun',
   'パスカル。圧力の SI 単位、記号 Pa。1気圧 ≈ 100 000 Pa、1 kPa = 1000 Pa。\nThe SI unit of pressure, symbol Pa. 1 atmosphere ≈ 100 000 Pa.',
   'Convert the pressure into pascals.\nA pressure of 101 kPa is 101 000 Pa.'],
  ['Volume Units', 'Expression',
   '体積の単位。1 m³ = 1000 dm³ = 1 000 000 cm³、1 dm³ = 1 L = 1000 cm³。\n1 m3 = 1000 dm3 = 1 000 000 cm3; 1 dm3 = 1 litre = 1000 cm3.',
   'Convert the volume units into cubic metres before using pV = nRT.\nRemember the volume units: 24 dm3 is 24 000 cm3.'],
  ['Calculate', 'Verb',
   '計算せよ。数値を出す設問。計算過程を示し、単位と有効数字に注意する。\nProduce a number. Show your working, carry the units through, and quote a sensible number of significant figures.',
   'Calculate the relative atomic mass of the element.\nCalculate the mass of copper produced, giving your answer to three significant figures.'],
  ['Deduce', 'Verb',
   '推定せよ。与えられた情報から論理的に答えを導く。暗記した知識を答えるのではない。\nWork the answer out from the information you are given, using reasoning.',
   'Deduce the formula of the compound from the data in the table.\nDeduce, with a reason, which element it is.'],
  ['Define', 'Verb',
   '定義を述べよ。覚えた定義を正確な言い回しで書く。各節に配点がある。\nGive the exact, learned wording of a definition. Every clause carries a mark.',
   'Define the term first ionisation energy.\nDefine relative atomic mass.'],
  ['Explain', 'Verb',
   '説明せよ。観察だけでなく理由を述べる。because、so、therefore を使う。\nGive a reason, not just an observation.',
   'Explain why the first ionisation energy decreases down the group.\nExplain, in terms of intermolecular forces, why water has a high boiling point.'],
  ['Suggest', 'Verb',
   '考えを述べよ。既知の知識を未知の状況に応用する。答えが複数あり得る。\nApply what you know to an unfamiliar situation. There may be more than one acceptable answer.',
   'Suggest why the actual yield is less than the theoretical yield.\nSuggest a reason for the peak at m/e = 29.'],
];

var MATH_WORDS = [
  ['Quadratic Equation', 'Noun',
   '二次方程式。ax² + bx + c = 0（a ≠ 0）の形の方程式。因数分解、平方完成、解の公式で解く。\nAn equation of the form ax2 + bx + c = 0 with a ≠ 0.',
   'Solve the quadratic equation 2x2 – 5x + 2 = 0.\nShow that the quadratic equation has no real roots.'],
